//? Title: ResNet Bottlenecks: Channel Compression, Expansion and Sampling
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Deep residual models need to keep expensive spatial filters affordable. Bottlenecks compress
channels before a 3×3 filter and expand them before shortcut addition. We inspect their exact
sampling rule, verify all five-stage geometries for ResNet-50/101/152, and fit each full-depth
compact topology with explicit tensor Adam updates and paired parameter/state checkpoints.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.3_residual_and_dense_families/resnet_bottleneck.cpp`.

> The method

A bottleneck applies bias-free 1×1 reduction, 3×3 spatial mixing and 1×1 expansion. The first
two use BN/ReLU; the last uses BN without ReLU. With inner width w, the block emits 4w
channels. Identity shortcuts apply only when the input already has that width and stride one;
other cases use normalized 1×1 projections. The sum is followed by ReLU, so zero residuals
still yield ReLU(shortcut), with negative-input clipping just as in the basic-block report.

For equal input/output width C=4w, the branch kernel cost is

$$
Cw+9w^2+wC=17w^2=\frac{17}{16}C^2,
$$
\alttext(The expensive spatial kernel operates at one-quarter of the outer channel width.)

Two basic 3×3 filters at outer width C would instead use 18 C² kernel weights. This compares
kernel arithmetic at the same outer width, excluding BN, shortcut projections, memory and
nonlinearities; the blocks have different capacity. For w=2 and C=8 the bottleneck has 68
kernel weights. Its learned pointwise reduction is a structural restriction, not a general
identity for every possible dense spatial transform.

Original bottlenecks place stage downsampling in the first 1×1 convolution. The optional
`stride_on_3x3` setting moves it to the spatial filter (often called v1.5). A pointwise stride-two
filter samples rows/columns 0,2,4 and cannot see an impulse at (1,1). A stride-one reduction
followed by stride-two 3×3 mixing does see it. The fixture shares weights, sets the shortcut
to zero and compensates inference BN scaling, producing zero versus a 2×2 patch of fours
in every output channel. This checks sampling, not a claim that either variant is always better.

ResNet-50/101/152 use block counts 3/4/6/3, 3/4/23/3 and 3/8/36/3. Learned depth equals
three per block plus stem and classifier, excluding projection shortcuts. Full 224-pixel
stages end at 56/28/14/7 with channels 256/512/1024/2048. Learned counts are
25557032, 44549160 and 60192808; separate moving-statistic counts are 53120, 105344 and
151424. The stem uses symmetric 7×7/2 padding and padded 3×3/2 max-pooling. BN learns
scale and offset with epsilon 1e-5 and eager unbiased-variance moving updates. No pretrained
weights or dropout are used. Full forward and compact training-gradient checks are distinct.

The dataset block keeps all layers, original bottleneck stride placement, one-eighth widths
and a 3×3 stride-one small-image stem. It fits 64 original CIFAR-10 images, validates on 32
and evaluates 32 official-test images only after selection. Five epochs use batch sixteen,
Adam(.001), explicit kernel L2=.0001/2 and independent tensor crop/flip augmentation. Both
parameter and moving-state checkpoints restore together. This four-stage CIFAR adaptation
is a bounded CPU demonstration, not the original ImageNet training recipe or a depth ranking.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/resnet_bottleneck_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/resnet_bottleneck_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/resnet_bottleneck_concept.tikz > build/resnet_bottleneck_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/resnet_bottleneck_concept.svg build/resnet_bottleneck_concept.dvi > build/resnet_bottleneck_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
